I will have to let @roji answer the question as to whether passing enumerated values to Npgsql v2 worked or not.  However, I do know that **passing the underlying integral type has never worked in Npgsql**, in either v2 or v3.  Therefore, even if you make the change for all versions of Npgsql, and Npgsql v2 doesn't support enumerations, it would still be broken in that version.  This guarantees that nobody is relying on existing behavior, so I personally don't think that changing it for all revisions of Npgsql would matter.  However, if you (or whomever implements it) would like to leave it alone in Npgsql v2, then you could modify the FeatureSupport class to be the following:

``` C#
/// <summary>
/// Handles variances in features per DBMS
/// </summary>
partial class FeatureSupport
{
    private static readonly FeatureSupport
        @default = new FeatureSupport(false),
        postgres2 = new FeatureSupport(true),
        postgres3 = new FeatureSupport(true, true);

    /// <summary>
    /// Gets the feature set based on the passed connection
    /// </summary>
    public static FeatureSupport Get(IDbConnection connection)
    {
        var connType = (connection == null ? null : connection.GetType());
        var typeName = (connType == null ? null : connType.Name);
        if (string.Equals(name, "npgsqlconnection", StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase))
        {
            var versionAttr = connType.Assembly
                .GetCustomAttribute<AssemblyFileVersionAttribute>();
            Version version;
            var isV3OrNewer = (versionAttr != null &&
                !string.IsNullOrEmpty(versionAttr.Version) &&
                Version.TryParse(versionAttr.Version, out version) &&
                3 <= version.Major);
            return (isV3OrNewer ? postgres3 : postgres2);
        }
        return @default;
    }

    private FeatureSupport(bool arrays, bool enums = false)
    {
        Arrays = arrays;
        Enums = enums;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// True if the db supports array columns e.g. Postgresql
    /// </summary>
    public bool Arrays { get; private set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// True if the db supports enumerated types e.g. Postgresql v3+
    /// </summary>
    public bool Enums { get; private set; }
}
```

Then, my proposed changes to `SqlMapper.LookupDbType` would simply become:

``` C#
// Existing conditional
if (type.IsEnum() && !typeMap.ContainsKey(type))
// Proposed change
if (FeatureSupport(conn).Enums && type.IsEnum() && !typeMap.ContainsKey(type))
```

And the following conditional in `SqlMapper.SanitizeParameterValue` would become:

``` C#
// Existing conditional
if (value is Enum)
// Proposed change
if (FeatureSupport(conn).Enums && value is Enum)
```

In addition, the following changes would need to be made:

> 1) The internal Identity class would need to store the connection passed to the constructor
> 2) The following public methods would need an additional IDbConnection parameter:
>      GetDbType(...)
>      Output&lt;T&gt;(...)
> 3) The following internal method would need an additional IDbConnection parameter:
>      LookupDbType(...)

My personal belief is that this is overly verbose, and incredibly complex given the nature of the issue.  This solution also requires changing the public interface of SqlMapper, which I don't particularly like.  I would much rather have the poor man's implementation done ASAP so Postgres users have some sort of enum support, rather than wait for some grandiose implementation that will probably never get done.  Alternatively, if issues #259 / #286 get resolved, then I would be OK waiting since there would at least be some way for us to override the default (non-working) behavior, despite it being very dirty.

An alternative implementation would be to refactor the existing enum code into its own TypeHandler, that could be overriden by adding our own.  This may be better than handling enumerated types with special, hard-coded handlers as is currently done.  For example, implement SqlMapper.TypeHandler&lt;Enum&gt;, and add that to the list of handlers.  Then, after checking whether a handler exists for a specific type, get the Enum handler if and only if the type is an enum type.  This would have the following added benefits:

> 1) It would resolve #259 / #286 at the same time
> 2) It would reduce the special cases in the code base
> 3) It would enable providing a custom handler for all enumerated types

I think this would be my favorite approach.  No special static members, and no changes to public interfaces.  If you want me to look at how the code would change for such an implementation, I would be more than happy to.

